#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096814 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096493 :question/id #uuid "6dfe09f0-30e2-53bc-a8f6-d7e4a3b8232a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5181" :question/problem "![](images/d3/d3042242136b82b867b8bd7911761ee38318924df0c9979488d20f04117c45d9.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf a$." :question/worked-solution "![](images/b3/b316448c885a20a7ed60871ce0f1a4dc4f77bd4658dc6eda3407fb3c89a088cb.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have\n\n$$\n\\angle AHB = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\n, $AH=3$ and $BH=2$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^{2} + BH^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3^{2} + 2^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{9 + 4} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{13}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$ is $\\sqrt{13}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{\\mid a \\mid} \\cdot a \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b11642dd-80cc-539c-8c2d-8cdee58e9d3e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049923 :topic/math-academy-id 1927 :topic/title "The Product Rule for Radicals"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050315 :topic/math-academy-id 2240 :topic/title "The Quotient Rule for Radicals"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097161 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae96c78f-e10d-563b-82af-e2c02160928e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{29}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094940} {:answer/id #uuid "25f7a90e-939a-568d-ba3d-07a616fcb021" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{q + r}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094941} {:answer/id #uuid "328e6ad2-ef2f-5827-8881-60cdfaebb76b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2q}{\\sqrt{13}} + \\frac{r}{\\sqrt{29}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094942} {:answer/id #uuid "681555fa-cdb3-5281-b943-0cf1eed80c35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2(q + r))/(\\sqrt{73})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094943} {:answer/id #uuid "703e463a-8826-5adf-9d3d-36bfd8037d83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{q + r}{\\sqrt{13}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094944}] :answer-field/id #uuid "96962338-e491-50f7-b8da-1efc8b69ba83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094939}] :question/id #uuid "9d05ca0e-131b-5294-8367-1760396e4369" :question/math-academy-id "q-19276" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9dfaf74a497a2fd22428e09a4c26d573e184c9728ccf67f11ebb9d6a2a44f4c6.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $q + r$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097177 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8688d259-91a0-57b9-a118-ec71feec2112" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{65}}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095036} {:answer/id #uuid "a1324d04-e706-5a76-a501-88a91adb3e11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{55}}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095037} {:answer/id #uuid "228bdee3-07e5-5a7d-9a23-1d3da2c66da2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{65}}{4}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095038} {:answer/id #uuid "44ddca2e-c35f-567f-9191-68cb552e7650" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{55}}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095039} {:answer/id #uuid "4741a424-03b7-5a24-97e3-1e5c43226795" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{65}}c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095040}] :answer-field/id #uuid "943eaa96-2961-5fec-9654-295f42c3d887" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095035}] :question/id #uuid "8ddbfd9c-fd49-551e-8c5c-9bdbb0fc04fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-20407" :question/problem "![](images/13/1319298a994d5daf26853baf7d255490827ada4123debdf0b4374db1ed03f7af.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $c$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233276 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "45f48cf6-e519-5407-abc6-4bbd59c8789a" :question/math-academy-id "q-119805" :question/problem "![](images/95/95c30eab094a24beb6976262167401efa973dbac12781c69d074eb14e12e2743.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $m+n?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $m+n.$ According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $m+n$ goes from the tail of $m$ to the head of $n:$ \n\n![](images/87/87647211f77776c5f5f740c205fafb150eef1b9d3ebefb82df73a9fdcd953bdb.png)\n\nIn $△AHB$, we have $∠AHB={90}^{∘}$, $AH=0.5=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $BH=2.$ So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|m+n| & =AB \\\\  & =\\sqrt{A{H}^{2}+B{H}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $m+n$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}.$ Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{1}{|m+n|}\\cdot (m+n) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}}(m+n).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Unit Vector Parallel to a Given Vector in a Diagram"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096815 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096448 :question/id #uuid "23d9a601-974b-51c2-a6ea-0ebadde19efc" :question/math-academy-id "e-4283" :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ is directed horizontally to the right and has magnitude $10$, while the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is directed vertically down and has magnitude $20$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a+b}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $\\mathbf{a+b}$. According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}$ goes from the tail of $\\mathbf{a}$ to the head of $\\mathbf{b}$:\n\n![](images/32/32c48d8c19fc68fbc172b240ded922f3e2ebc12804411c318ff63fe999aaedcc.png)\n\nTo find the unit vector corresponding to $\\mathbf{a+b}$, we have to divide by the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{a + b}{\\mid a + b \\mid}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the magnitude, first notice that we obtain a right triangle. So, we can use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}a + b & ^{2} &= & a & ^{2} +\\left.\\end{vmatrix}b\\right|^{2} \\\\\n&= 10^{2} + 20^{2} \\\\\n&= 100 + 400 \\\\\n&= 500\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\mathbf{a+b} &= \\sqrt{500}=10\\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{a + b}{\\mid a + b \\mid} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{10\\sqrt{5}}(a + b)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d44ac67b-5e3d-5b1e-bf2c-554b592ba29e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097042 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab0413fa-4042-5147-a3d8-818097bc2626" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}(a + b)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094226} {:answer/id #uuid "c81ea504-05ac-5c44-a92e-1630193abb8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(a + b)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094227} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b9b147-1585-55ec-b881-e10cd196e800" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}(a + b)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094228} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5dad94-0917-544d-85f3-4e70f0e93daf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{3}}(a + b)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094229} {:answer/id #uuid "24b28af8-0f33-5293-a88e-61ee2a659e20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}(a + b)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094230}] :answer-field/id #uuid "bd239bbe-a8f9-5077-a835-d71f38e1b449" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094225}] :question/id #uuid "3f0752f9-cfba-5496-a6dc-d603b64b1e81" :question/math-academy-id "q-12184" :question/problem "The vector $a$ is directed horizontally to the right and has magnitude $3$, while the vector $b$ is directed vertically down and has magnitude $4$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $a + b$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097043 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c27fb23c-7e75-5962-96da-f5d4cdf55baa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{569}}(a + b)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094232} {:answer/id #uuid "e7540039-cc90-5f35-b9e5-f11de4ba13e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{573}}(a + b)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094233} {:answer/id #uuid "2d97d629-615d-5343-ae83-2b49d9c22d39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{561}}(a + b)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094234} {:answer/id #uuid "3938b713-9ab0-5b89-b60b-79de69627c20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{23}(a + b)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094235} {:answer/id #uuid "521a0f0f-f648-5c38-afda-9cbb447db899" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}(a + b)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094236}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5e4bfff2-68a6-59a4-831d-e2298ec06fa4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094231}] :question/id #uuid "d188cb86-3193-5752-99d0-e273954248f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-12185" :question/problem "The vector $a$ is directed due north-east and $\\mid a|= 20$, while the vector $b$ is directed due south-east and $\\mid b|= 13$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $a + b$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Unit Vector Parallel to a Vector Sum"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096841 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096400 :question/id #uuid "19bf8d06-cbcc-547b-b882-ac89cd39fd1a" :question/math-academy-id "e-181" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = (3x^4+x^3)^5,$ calculate $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf = g^5, \\qquad g = 3x^4+x^3\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g}  = 5g^4,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x} = 12x^3 + 3x^2.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =5{g}^{4}\\cdot (12{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =5(3{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}{)}^{4}(12{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29956ecb-a28a-5406-b8dc-9776e9a3ccee" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048932 :topic/math-academy-id 278 :topic/title "The Sum and Constant Multiple Rules for Differentiation"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049317 :topic/math-academy-id 3817 :topic/title "Describing Function Composition"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050644 :topic/math-academy-id 986 :topic/title "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Line Using Differentiation"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097250 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85752216-72d1-52c5-a307-1f7593bb2baa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x^{4} + 3x)(4x^{3} + 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095425} {:answer/id #uuid "bffd2b2f-d555-5e17-8756-8a754febf526" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x^{4} + 3x)(4x^{3} + 3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095426} {:answer/id #uuid "eef892e3-6947-592f-9345-311b595e0536" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(4x^{3})(x^{4} + 3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095427} {:answer/id #uuid "05327ffd-b783-558f-8116-edad45453171" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(4x^{3})(x^{4} + 3x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095428} {:answer/id #uuid "62baa9f7-a4b9-5ff4-84f6-37fdf189f3fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x^{4} + 3x)(4x^{3} + 3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095429}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a72fa786-3004-51a5-a233-17a9a216dfb3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095424}] :question/id #uuid "b551e489-4c37-53ea-831b-8f3ca007450b" :question/math-academy-id "q-48625" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{df}{dx}$ when $f(x) = (x^{4} + 3x)^{2}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097325 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c2e9e80-9009-5c4d-8418-53807b048413" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18(4+6x{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333097} {:answer/id #uuid "2f451149-34e3-5874-ac22-67e5f7f806cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18x(4+6x{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333429} {:answer/id #uuid "ac1f9842-ca0a-55db-bcb6-06735b728dba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(4+6x{)}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334496} {:answer/id #uuid "b1df2cc8-3e8e-59c7-a92f-f4f573b06622" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(4+6x{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334551} {:answer/id #uuid "d848947b-033e-583b-9707-793c5c4bf6dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30(4+6x{)}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c2e9e80-9009-5c4d-8418-53807b048413" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18(4+6x{)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333097} :answer-field/id #uuid "564e943b-61e3-5dd9-b71b-ad37ff586952" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335411}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f0bd56a8-5c99-5166-be8e-4185bfb62ed5" :question/math-academy-id "q-666" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ when $f(x)=(4+6x{)}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={g}^{3},\\,g=4+6x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=3{g}^{2},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=6.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =3\\cdot 6\\cdot (4+6x{)}^{2} \\\\  & =18(4+6x{)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235382 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "182d03f6-7007-5d30-9f41-468940bf1b5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8t(2{t}^{2}+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "182d03f6-7007-5d30-9f41-468940bf1b5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8t(2{t}^{2}+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234577} :answer-field/id #uuid "88d80e90-19de-5aa6-bcad-6a5bb1a4fd17" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235141}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5f760aed-6b31-5953-b3f9-00dcac8b3038" :question/math-academy-id "q-132974" :question/problem "$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}]=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's define a function $h(t):$\n\n$$\nh(t)=(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}\n$$\n\nTo differentiate this function, we use the chain rule. Let \n\n$$\ng(t)=2{t}^{2}+3,\\,h(g(t))=g(t)^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating our functions, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =(2{t}^{2}+3)^{′}=4t, \\\\ {h}^{′}(g(t)) & =({g}^{2})^{′}=2g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}] & ={h}^{′}(t) \\\\  & ={h}^{′}(g(t))\\cdot {g}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =2g\\cdot 4t \\\\  & =8t(2{t}^{2}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250188 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "147b8607-00ab-583f-9d29-c36db3ffb93e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30{(5x+10)}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247609} {:answer/id #uuid "8b9923c0-68fb-5db2-a270-b22a0a565a94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30{(5x+10)}^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248673} {:answer/id #uuid "b955e3b8-b464-5b10-97ae-eea2d8b024ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{(5x+10)}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249080} {:answer/id #uuid "efb057ba-bc50-5a4b-9c8c-026e21fb0356" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10{(5x+10)}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249528} {:answer/id #uuid "f825b128-d786-54b0-9c93-577427b75d05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(5x+10)}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "147b8607-00ab-583f-9d29-c36db3ffb93e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30{(5x+10)}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247609} :answer-field/id #uuid "1beb6b75-3505-5fda-ac7d-3d7a94737480" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249718}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "421599a5-1cf3-595e-9d3b-cce9b649fa80" :question/math-academy-id "q-11410" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ when $f(x)={(5x+10)}^{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={g}^{6},\\,g=5x+10\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=6{g}^{5},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=5.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =6{g}^{5}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =30(5x+10{)}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314997 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db185f31-d898-509f-b9c4-a2881a9060fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-28(1-7x)^3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313696}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db185f31-d898-509f-b9c4-a2881a9060fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-28(1-7x)^3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313696} :answer-field/id #uuid "36ad8472-0eaf-547f-a51d-d9fd88b94c6d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314156}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "45dae178-5aad-56cb-99e8-4cae2859e574" :question/math-academy-id "q-340690" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ when $f(x)=(1-7x)^{4}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={g}^{4},\\,g=1-7x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=4{g}^{3},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=-7.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =4{g}^{3}\\cdot (-7) \\\\  & =-28(1-7x)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317301 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dddace1-6902-5bd8-8d9c-4f8e9ee4c695" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9dddace1-6902-5bd8-8d9c-4f8e9ee4c695" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316001} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb310ac7-3d59-5c34-90b1-fa1d08886eef" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317143}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f5911b4d-cb67-58a7-a1eb-0368289d94d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-342630" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={({x}^{2}+2x+1)}^{2},$ the slope at the point $(0,1)$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\nFirst, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={g}^{2},\\,g={x}^{2}+2x+1\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=2g,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=2x+2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =2g\\cdot (2x+2) \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+2x+1)(2x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line at the point where $x=0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & =[2({x}^{2}+2x+1)(2x+2){]}_{x=0} \\\\  & =2((0{)}^{2}+2\\cdot (0)+1)(2\\cdot (0)+2) \\\\  & =2(1)(2) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317308 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afdc11cd-82dd-5c6f-bcb3-29722512bae8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316117}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afdc11cd-82dd-5c6f-bcb3-29722512bae8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316117} :answer-field/id #uuid "466922a0-7e46-579f-8acd-8a627c38b832" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316705}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d9eda9c-9861-5891-97dc-ba4b870f7db4" :question/math-academy-id "q-342663" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={(3x+1)}^{4},$ the slope at the point $(-1,16)$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use chain rule to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\nFirst, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={g}^{4},\\,g=3x+1\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=4{g}^{3},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=3.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =4{g}^{3}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12(3x+1{)}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line at the point where $x=-1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-1} & =[12(3x+1{)}^{3}{]}_{x=-1} \\\\  & =12(3\\cdot (-1)+1{)}^{3} \\\\  & =12(-2{)}^{3} \\\\  & =-96.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317337 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e15c4f3-e0aa-5296-8996-2def04133195" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6x+11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316002}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e15c4f3-e0aa-5296-8996-2def04133195" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6x+11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316002} :answer-field/id #uuid "adb706a4-b97c-5d9a-a3d9-b9ec8f4ae7e2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316955}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f16d8b27-8720-58fb-ad21-d69be0217bc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-342910" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=(3-2x{)}^{3},$ the equation of the tangent at the point $(2,-1)$ is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\nFirst, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={g}^{3},\\,g=3-2x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=3{g}^{2},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=-2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =3{g}^{2}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-6(3-2x{)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line at the point where $x=2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=2} & =(-6{(3-2x)}^{2}){|}_{x=2} \\\\  & =-6{(3-2(2))}^{2} \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line at the point $(2,-1)$ is $m=-6.$ Now, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+1 & =-6\\cdot (x-2) \\\\ y & =-6x+12-1 \\\\ y & =-6x+11\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329904 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "354142ae-6999-5699-b81d-9f16570b6115" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-12x+25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327394} {:answer/id #uuid "6637b75c-a39e-5721-a28b-4a320ad5c971" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=12x-23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327821} {:answer/id #uuid "790d805f-3881-5904-ba81-7ea14a53c052" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3x+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327971} {:answer/id #uuid "85aa1fa8-f1d2-5839-ba72-e02c15010a7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328087} {:answer/id #uuid "bcf17a1f-abef-5a3c-bb11-4d68176c30e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4x-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6637b75c-a39e-5721-a28b-4a320ad5c971" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=12x-23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327821} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f94fd28-0c54-584b-93ad-2ea2c59a1f97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329394}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6c44e7b4-d61a-54f6-b052-1c62e5ff035f" :question/math-academy-id "q-48666" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={(5-3x)}^{4},$ find the equation of the tangent at point $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\nFirst, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={g}^{4},\\,g=5-3x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=4{g}^{3},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=-3.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =4{g}^{3}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-12(5-3x)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line at the point where $x=2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=2} & =-12{(5-3(2))}^{3} \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line at the point $(2,1)$ is $m=12.$ Now, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =12(x-2) \\\\ y-1 & =12x-24 \\\\ y & =12x-23\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Polynomial Raised to a Power Using the Chain Rule"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096842 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096401 :question/id #uuid "8fcdb2c8-71ca-5bfa-b7fe-49f8f2c05bbb" :question/math-academy-id "e-182" :question/problem "Given that $y = \\sqrt{1-x^2},$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}.\n$$\n\nHere, we've used $u$ instead of $g$ for the intermediate variable. \n\nLet $u=1-x^2.$ Then $y = \\sqrt u = u^{1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\dfrac{1}{2}u^{-1/2} = \\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt u}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}  = -2x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-2x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{2\\sqrt{u}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f0cdac9-57b2-5634-8d98-d6adf8a85204" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097251 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "256d8104-e561-50d4-bb47-c84cda5f979d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{(5x-1{)}^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327259} {:answer/id #uuid "92909c43-4d9f-55c2-9367-b2903d4d10f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328210} {:answer/id #uuid "b84fdb58-69a3-5339-b857-edeff32c5507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5x-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328539} {:answer/id #uuid "ccdb8149-860b-5213-8db1-d92c7a922a29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{5x-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328733} {:answer/id #uuid "e32be03e-6bf9-5893-a620-899e6e49abe1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{(5x-1{)}^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "256d8104-e561-50d4-bb47-c84cda5f979d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{(5x-1{)}^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327259} :answer-field/id #uuid "14eebaf9-3e88-5f2b-82c3-330efcb42e3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329219}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "854ec833-4bc0-54f0-9210-c708a9342fef" :question/math-academy-id "q-48730" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5x-1}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=5x-1.$ Then $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}={u}^{-1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\cdot (5) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{(5x-1{)}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097258 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d468059-f4b7-51ec-a214-229418cddeaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{4x - 3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095473} {:answer/id #uuid "ce172954-02de-5948-98e6-063d373c04b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4x - 3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095474} {:answer/id #uuid "dd38fa2b-33de-5df5-aa85-ed357c5eb5e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4x + 3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095475} {:answer/id #uuid "fd120f87-d6a9-56e7-ae4f-db302afd2580" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4x + 3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095476} {:answer/id #uuid "7f7778bb-8979-5802-bf93-4a409e6e58b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4x - 3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095477}] :answer-field/id #uuid "84ab9969-a822-5aa8-beea-30e48f66c60f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095472}] :question/id #uuid "ea97309c-a67a-5e7f-aa97-023276ac6669" :question/math-academy-id "q-48846" :question/problem "Given that $y = \\sqrt{4x - 3}$, find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244040 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27cec937-8f89-509c-89d8-cac2418dad31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12\\sqrt{3-6x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241525} {:answer/id #uuid "48530683-52e3-5b2d-b80c-52c96ea80021" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3-6x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241833} {:answer/id #uuid "6ea096e1-e11d-5b2f-9a38-2f0713ebe775" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{3-6x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242165} {:answer/id #uuid "afef1e77-e7bb-55d4-85fd-246b46e4f2ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3-6x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242713} {:answer/id #uuid "eb52bd30-42b5-564a-b65a-5b27e7b61017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3-6x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afef1e77-e7bb-55d4-85fd-246b46e4f2ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3-6x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242713} :answer-field/id #uuid "beb44a20-d166-5fe6-a7bc-aa72ceec3cb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243776}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0ce97e32-c8a9-5155-bb70-6cb7385360f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-107291" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\sqrt{3-6x},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=3-6x.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}={u}^{1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-6.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3-6x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244048 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b523831-f11c-5631-b1d0-55d429e9b570" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241281} {:answer/id #uuid "321ef9b3-dc8b-5b99-b689-dfe8d1c4cf58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241620} {:answer/id #uuid "501d0fb0-7ccb-548e-bb3a-b18b59d46b6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241894} {:answer/id #uuid "8cf3df5e-c66f-522b-838e-722473ef4ed3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242424} {:answer/id #uuid "99c71189-09eb-555b-8519-9b447439afc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "501d0fb0-7ccb-548e-bb3a-b18b59d46b6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241894} :answer-field/id #uuid "19320ca1-b053-535e-b992-542e4931ff17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243482}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c8b46779-d19f-5cca-987d-5f500a9de334" :question/math-academy-id "q-107360" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{1-4x}$ at the point where $x=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=1-4x.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{1-4x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-2} & ={(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{1-4x}})|}_{x=-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{1-4(-2)}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297398 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb26afef-455b-5297-b622-9e232fa102cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2x+7)^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb26afef-455b-5297-b622-9e232fa102cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2x+7)^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296332} :answer-field/id #uuid "69dfefe3-2eb8-5512-ab7d-eb2788925109" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296855}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2300bd8f-e3d1-544c-8ad1-127cb2f0bedc" :question/math-academy-id "q-314194" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x+7}}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=2x+7.$ Then $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}={u}^{-1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2x+7{)}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317312 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3cdb155-0bd1-58bc-961e-1bfa7707dad6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-6x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3cdb155-0bd1-58bc-961e-1bfa7707dad6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-6x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316217} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd768cbb-f716-59e5-acc7-6f058b68d4ef" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317150}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1f8a23e9-b484-592c-83ed-60ed3cc8b897" :question/math-academy-id "q-342680" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-6x}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u={x}^{2}-6x.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x-6.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (2x-6) \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x-3)}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-6x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-6x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317313 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8316ace3-a7b2-57fb-a2e0-d20a2908d622" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3x+6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315844}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8316ace3-a7b2-57fb-a2e0-d20a2908d622" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3x+6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315844} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7c696cc-7f33-5839-8d2a-38d27e70f63c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316978}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c93d262c-d38e-53b1-ba9c-9af520b2e82c" :question/math-academy-id "q-342683" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\sqrt{3x+6}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=3x+6.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3x+6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317321 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea0b074e-9124-53da-b940-4a0beab23775" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316419}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea0b074e-9124-53da-b940-4a0beab23775" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316419} :answer-field/id #uuid "cae0bf8f-009f-5cd9-a958-5250470d215a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317025}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "65a56fb3-c803-55e2-851b-d52f4cc805e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-342764" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5x+1}}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=5x+1.$ Then $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}={u}^{-1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317326 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22c57943-65ff-5d5d-81f8-1002a59bb417" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22c57943-65ff-5d5d-81f8-1002a59bb417" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315311} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e7bce52-e2d7-50a5-a44d-84c8a8cc1d7d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316728}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "febc6c25-71ff-51d9-b851-6f19e65ebcd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-342802" :question/problem "The slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}+12x}}$ at the point where $x=2$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=3{x}^{2}+12x.$ Then $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}={u}^{-1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6x+12.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\cdot (6x+12) \\\\  & =-\\frac{6x+12}{2\\sqrt{(3{x}^{2}+12x{)}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=2} & ={(-\\frac{6x+12}{2\\sqrt{(3{x}^{2}+12x{)}^{3}}})|}_{x=2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6(2)+12}{2\\sqrt{(3(2{)}^{2}+12(2){)}^{3}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{24}{2\\sqrt{{36}^{3}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Radical Function Using the Chain Rule"}]}
